  1376                              <1> ; =============================================================================
  1377                              <1> ;  The line editor (EDITOR = 1)
  1378                              <1> ;
  1379                              <1> ;  The interpreter starts here, and a program that ends comes back here.
  1380                              <1> ;  There is no prompt. It follows the spec's suggested standard for a line
  1381                              <1> ;  editor (docs/b8script.md, Environments), and is the Z80's editor, key for
  1382                              <1> ;  key. Type a line and press Return:
  1383                              <1> ;
  1384                              <1> ;    n text    put the text in as line n; the lines from n on move down. A
  1385                              <1> ;              number past the end (or 0) adds the line at the end.
  1386                              <1> ;    n         take line n out
  1387                              <1> ;    anything else is run at once, as B8Script. (Start such a line with a
  1388                              <1> ;    space if it would begin with a digit.)
  1389                              <1> ;
  1390                              <1> ;  A command key ends the line too, and takes what was typed as its argument:
  1391                              <1> ;
  1392                              <1> ;    ^G        run the program
  1393                              <1> ;    n ^L      list from line n (no number: all of it). Any key holds the
  1394                              <1> ;              list until another key is pressed; Ctrl-C ends it.
  1395                              <1> ;    n ^E      put line n in the input line; Return puts it back, changed
  1396                              <1> ;    name ^R   read the file of that name (no name: the current one: the
  1397                              <1> ;              one from the command line, or the last one typed)
  1398                              <1> ;    name ^W   write the program to that file (no name: the current one)
  1399                              <1> ;    ^N ^N     new: no program (the key twice in a row)
  1400                              <1> ;    ^X        leave
  1401                              <1> ;
  1402                              <1> ;  Any other control key does nothing.
  1403                              <1> ;
  1404                              <1> ;  In the line: ^B and ^F, or the left and right arrow keys, move the cursor;
  1405                              <1> ;  a character goes in at the cursor; Backspace and DEL take out the one
  1406                              <1> ;  before it; Esc drops the line (after Esc it waits 5 ms for a '[': Esc [ C
  1407                              <1> ;  and Esc [ D are right and left too). Only CR, LF, BS and printing
  1408                              <1> ;  characters are sent to the terminal.
  1409                              <1> ;
  1410                              <1> ;  Lines are counted, not numbered. A typed line holds 125 characters; the
  1411                              <1> ;  one character after a line number separates it from the text and is not
  1412                              <1> ;  stored. The text is kept as typed, each line ending in CR LF, at PROG. A
  1413                              <1> ;  file is read up to its first ^Z, and a last line with no line end is
  1414                              <1> ;  given one; it is written as it is, with no padding.
  1415                              <1> ;
  1416                              <1> ;  What lasts from one line to the next: the program, the words, the
  1417                              <1> ;  variables and the data stack (TOS is kept pushed while the editor runs).
  1418                              <1> ;  An error or a break clears both stacks. The return stack starts empty for
  1419                              <1> ;  every line.
  1420                              <1> ;
  1421                              <1> ;  Registers in the editor: EBX = the cursor, EDX = the end of the line
  1422                              <1> ;  (pointers into the line buffer); EDI is free (edit resets it).
  1423                              <1> ; =============================================================================
  1424                              <1> %if EDITOR
  1425                              <1> ; ^E: line n goes into the input line, to be changed. V_EDLN remembers
  1426                              <1> ; which line it was: Return puts the text back in its place.
  1427                              <1> cedln:  CALLP   rdnum
  1427                              <2> cedln: 
   192                              <2>  %if P_%1
